<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John Calvin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1509">1509</a>–<a href="/years/1564">1564</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Jean Calvin</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John Calvin was a French theologian and reformer whose Institutes of the Christian Religion became a founding text of Reformed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestantism">Protestantism</a>. He arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> in 1536, was expelled in 1538 and returned in 1541, writing the church ordinances and working with the Consistory to discipline the city's morals. Geneva became a model and training centre for Reformed churches across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-calvin.jpg" alt="John Calvin" width="320" height="444" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous (France)Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:John_Calvin_Museum_Catharijneconvent_RMCC_s84_cropped.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>John Calvin was born Jehan Cauvin on 10 July 1509 at Noyon, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picardy">Picardy</a>, where his father Gérard was the cathedral notary and intended his sons for the priesthood. He was educated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, trained as a humanist lawyer and published a commentary on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seneca-people">Seneca</a>'s De Clementia in 1532. He broke from the Roman Catholic Church around 1530. After violence against Protestants in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> he fled to Basel, where in March 1536 he published the first edition of the Institutes of the Christian Religion.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guillaume-farel">William Farel</a> recruited him for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>, but the city council resisted their proposals and both men were expelled. At <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-bucer">Martin Bucer</a>'s invitation Calvin became minister of a French refugee church in Strasbourg. Invited back to Geneva in 1541, he saw the council pass the Ecclesiastical Ordinances on 20 November 1541, which defined four orders of ministry and created the Consistory, a court of elders and ministers with no civil jurisdiction. Opposition came from wealthy, interrelated families he called libertines, led by Ami Perrin from about 1546.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Works and ideas</h2><p>Michael Servetus, a Spanish physician whose book rejected the Trinity, arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a> on 13 August 1553 and was burned at the stake for heresy by the city council after Calvin denounced him. Sebastian Castellio broke with Calvin over the treatment of heretics. In January 1555 the council confirmed that the Consistory should regain its powers, and Calvin's authority was practically uncontested in his last years. The final edition of the Institutes appeared in 1559, in four books and eighty chapters. His theology included predestination and God's absolute <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> in salvation, and his critics such as Jérôme-Hermès Bolsec attacked the first.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Calvin preached his last sermon on 6 February 1564 and died on 27 May 1564, aged 54. He was buried in an unmarked grave in the Cimetière des Rois. Geneva sheltered English exiles from 1555, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-knox">John Knox</a>, who carried his ideas to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. His reforms reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a> and the Palatinate, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heidelberg-catechism">Heidelberg Catechism</a> was written in 1563.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “John Calvin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His successor Heinrich Bullinger, head of the Zürich church from 1531 to 1575, corresponded across Europe, agreed the Consensus Tigurinus with <b>John Calvin</b> in 1549 and wrote the Second Helvetic Confession of 1566, adopted by Reformed churches from Hungary to Scotland.</blockquote><a href="/empire/canton-of-zurich">Canton of Zürich · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Huldrych Zwingli began reform in Zürich from 1519, and the allied city of Geneva, where <b>John Calvin</b> worked from 1536 and founded his academy in 1559, became the centre of Reformed Protestantism, whose ideas spread to France, the Netherlands, Scotland, Hungary and the English-speaking world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/old-swiss-confederacy">Old Swiss Confederacy · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Reformers Martin Luther and <b>John Calvin</b> wrote works that defined Protestant theology, and the Council of Trent, from 1545 to 1563, defined Catholic teaching in response.</blockquote><a href="/religion/christianity">Christianity · Texts &amp; Teachers</a></li><li><blockquote><b>John Calvin</b>, whose Institutes of the Christian Religion first appeared in 1536, stressed the sovereignty of God, including his predestination of those to be saved, and a church governed by pastors and elders that supervised the morals of its members.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Martin Luther translated the New Testament into German from Erasmus’s Greek text, and Huldrych Zwingli, Melanchthon and <b>John Calvin</b> were humanists by training, as were many Catholic reformers.</blockquote><a href="/movement/renaissance-humanism">Renaissance humanism · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote>The Dominican friar Girolamo Savonarola dominated Florence from 1494 to 1498; Anabaptists ruled Münster as a “New Jerusalem” until its prince-bishop retook the city in 1535; and historians still debate whether <b>John Calvin</b>’s Geneva, where a consistory of ministers and elders supervised morals from 1541, was a theocracy.</blockquote><a href="/government/theocracy">Theocracy · Through History</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Noyon, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/geneva">Geneva</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=49.58,3&amp;year=1544" aria-label="Noyon on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of John Calvin"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5166 5882h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5166 5882h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=49.58,3&amp;year=1544">What was here in 1544? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;True and substantial wisdom principally consists of two parts, the knowledge of God, and the knowledge of ourselves.&#8221;</p><footer>Institutes of the Christian Religion, book 1, chapter 1 &#183; tr. John Allen, 1813 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45001/pg45001-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…whithersoever you turn your eyes, there is not an atom of the world in which you cannot behold some brilliant sparks at least of his glory.&#8221;</p><footer>Institutes of the Christian Religion, book 1, chapter 5 &#183; tr. John Allen, 1813 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45001/pg45001-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Whence we may infer, that the mind of man is, if I may be allowed the expression, a perpetual manufactory of idols.&#8221;</p><footer>Institutes of the Christian Religion, book 1, chapter 11 &#183; tr. John Allen, 1813 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45001/pg45001-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><details class="enc-q-more"><summary>3 more</summary><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;For every man's understanding is like a labyrinth to him; so that it is not to be wondered at, that the different nations were drawn aside into various inventions…&#8221;</p><footer>Institutes of the Christian Religion, book 1, chapter 5 &#183; tr. John Allen, 1813 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45001/pg45001-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Just as if the world, which is a theatre erected for displaying the glory of God, were its own creator!&#8221;</p><footer>Institutes of the Christian Religion, book 1, chapter 5 &#183; tr. John Allen, 1813 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45001/pg45001-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;…so the Scripture, collecting in our minds the otherwise confused notions of Deity, dispels the darkness, and gives us a clear view of the true God.&#8221;</p><footer>Institutes of the Christian Religion, book 1, chapter 6 &#183; tr. John Allen, 1813 &#183; after likening Scripture to spectacles that let dim eyes read &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/45001/pg45001-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote></details><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
